9
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:26,600
Good afternoon, everyone.

10
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:28,120
My name is Neema Nassir.

11
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:31,520
I'm a postdoctoral associate at Transit Lab, MIT.

12
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:36,120
I've been invited to give a few lectures in this course

13
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:41,680
and basically participate in delivering the course material

14
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:42,560
for this course.

15
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:49,440
I will be talking in this session and in the next session

16
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:52,120
on short-range planning practice.

17
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:56,120
And then I'll be back again in April

18
00:00:56,120 --> 00:01:01,000
in four sessions in April, lecture number 13, 14, 15,

19
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,080
and 16 dealing with vehicle scheduling,

20
00:01:03,080 --> 00:01:05,200
crew scheduling, network and route structure,

21
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:11,880
and high ridership corridors.

22
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:15,120
So let's go ahead and start.

23
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:20,120
In this session, we're going to talk about short-range planning

24
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:21,440
practice.

25
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:25,920
And basically, I will give a brief set of definition

26
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:29,960
and concepts and introduction to the planning activities

27
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:33,160
that are usually made at transit authorities in the US

28
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:37,040
and also worldwide.

29
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:40,920
And then we will briefly talk about the measures and standards

30
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:44,560
and guidelines that are published and used

31
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:51,720
by transit agencies to plan their service based on those.

32
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:55,600
Then we will also talk about current practice

33
00:01:55,600 --> 00:02:00,800
and critique of the existing systems.

34
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:05,800
Probably, we will cover item one and some material

35
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:07,640
on item two this week.

36
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:13,200
And then the rest will actually be delivered to you

37
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:15,120
in our session next Tuesday.

38
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:26,200
So the planning decisions and planning activities

39
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:30,760
that are usually done at the transportation planning

40
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:33,240
agencies and public transport planning agencies

41
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:39,000
usually can be classified into four different type of decisions,

42
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,600
depending on frequency of decision making

43
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:44,120
and the need for making decisions.

44
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:50,880
And the scope of decisions, the scope of changes

45
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:53,960
that are related to the decisions that are made

46
00:02:53,960 --> 00:02:56,640
with each activity, these planning activities

47
00:02:56,640 --> 00:03:01,160
are divided into four different classes.

48
00:03:01,160 --> 00:03:05,600
Long-range planning activities, medium-range, short-range,

49
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:07,640
and control.

50
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:10,360
Long-range planning activities basically

51
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:13,480
include major capital investment,

52
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:16,560
major improvements to the infrastructure, which

53
00:03:16,560 --> 00:03:21,640
would include construction of a new station, a new rail line,

54
00:03:21,640 --> 00:03:24,080
a new busway to the system.

55
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:28,840
Or it could also be related to and as a result

56
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:31,560
of major institutional changes and changes

57
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:41,200
to the financial resources of the organization.

58
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:44,480
The decisions that involve long-range planning

59
00:03:44,480 --> 00:03:49,760
are usually driven by political and economic considerations.

60
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:53,520
And basically, the type of analysis

61
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:57,480
and the type of planning that is done in long-range planning

62
00:03:57,480 --> 00:04:01,520
activities involve projection into the future

63
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:06,880
and prediction of demand and prediction of situation

64
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:07,880
in the future.

65
00:04:07,880 --> 00:04:10,760
Therefore, it requires a comprehensive analysis,

66
00:04:10,760 --> 00:04:16,960
usually, for a strategic planning of interactions

67
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:20,120
between land use and changes in land use and interaction

68
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:22,440
between land use and demand in the future.

69
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:27,960
And it basically needs to capture or have an insight

70
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:34,520
into the future of the network and of the region.

71
00:04:34,520 --> 00:04:38,720
The next range is basically or the next class

72
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:42,760
of planning activities is basically

73
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:45,000
the medium-range activities.

74
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,080
Medium-range activities include bus network structure design,

75
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:52,560
network size design and improvement,

76
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:55,000
fleet size, fair policy, and technology.

77
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:02,160
It basically involves incremental or medium improvement

78
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:11,280
to the system and to the transit service.

79
00:05:11,280 --> 00:05:14,080
One point that I need to mention about the long-range

80
00:05:14,080 --> 00:05:19,120
and medium-range is that these two, although really important

81
00:05:19,120 --> 00:05:23,320
in the planning practice for transportation systems,

82
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:28,240
but these decisions are usually done at the upper level,

83
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:32,120
at the higher level than the transit authority.

84
00:05:32,120 --> 00:05:34,200
For example, long-range decisions

85
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:41,080
are usually done in collaboration between MPOs,

86
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:43,640
metropolitan planning organizations,

87
00:05:43,640 --> 00:05:45,520
state departments of transportation,

88
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:46,920
and transit authorities.

89
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:49,520
So transit authority is not the final decision-maker

90
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:53,400
in that case, and it needs close interaction

91
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:58,200
and close collaboration with the higher-level organizations

92
00:05:58,200 --> 00:06:01,280
and governmental agencies to make the final decisions.

93
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:02,600
So that's about the long-range.

94
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:04,800
Medium-range is also somehow similar.

95
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:06,480
It usually, for example, decisions

96
00:06:06,480 --> 00:06:09,280
that are related to the network structure,

97
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:13,600
although there is a lot of interesting research going on

98
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:16,720
in universities that deals with optimization

99
00:06:16,720 --> 00:06:18,640
of network design and optimization

100
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:20,440
of the structure of the network,

101
00:06:20,440 --> 00:06:25,080
because what happens in practice is that usually

102
00:06:25,080 --> 00:06:28,400
in the networks that we live and in current cities,

103
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:31,360
the actual layout and structure of the network

104
00:06:31,360 --> 00:06:32,760
is already given.

105
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:36,680
And it's usually rare or impossible

106
00:06:36,680 --> 00:06:40,920
that we start from scratch and start to design a network

107
00:06:40,920 --> 00:06:44,400
from scratch to begin with.

108
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:47,640
So therefore, what we usually observe

109
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:51,640
is incremental changes to the network addition

110
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:54,600
or elimination of some routes from the service

111
00:06:54,600 --> 00:06:58,000
and basically decisions that are related to that.

112
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:01,000
Yes, please.

113
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,400
In terms of fleet size, could that come

114
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:05,400
as a request from the agencies?

115
00:07:05,400 --> 00:07:07,320
An agency says, between the bus line,

116
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:10,920
it's at capacity or over capacity, we need more buses.

117
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:14,120
Could it come from a request to the higher level?

118
00:07:14,120 --> 00:07:15,680
Yes, definitely, yeah.

119
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:20,920
But it eventually requires the interaction

120
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:25,920
and also the securing of funding

121
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:28,680
from the upper level governmental agencies.

122
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:36,640
So the reason that I'm explaining this is

123
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:41,000
that I like for you to understand why we are focusing

124
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,120
on short range decision making

125
00:07:43,120 --> 00:07:46,160
and short range planning practice in this course.

126
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:50,040
That's mainly because the bulk part of activities

127
00:07:50,040 --> 00:07:54,760
at transit agencies is related to short range planning

128
00:07:54,760 --> 00:07:56,520
and short range decision making.

129
00:07:57,360 --> 00:08:01,000
So what we mean by short range decision making

130
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:04,960
are decisions that are usually made more frequently

131
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:07,600
than a yearly basis.

132
00:08:07,600 --> 00:08:10,880
And they basically entail decisions

133
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:13,840
about the structures of the route,

134
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:16,040
layout of the route, layout of the stops,

135
00:08:16,040 --> 00:08:21,040
and basically decisions about the frequency

136
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:26,400
of service, timetable, and vehicle and crew scheduling.

137
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:30,480
Despite the first two classes of,

138
00:08:31,480 --> 00:08:33,280
the top two classes of decision making

139
00:08:33,280 --> 00:08:38,040
that are usually driven by political

140
00:08:38,040 --> 00:08:42,280
and economic considerations, short range decision making

141
00:08:42,280 --> 00:08:47,280
and control activities are usually basically driven

142
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,200
by cost optimization.

143
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:55,200
And these are basically tactical decisions

144
00:08:55,200 --> 00:08:57,400
as opposed to strategic decisions

145
00:08:57,400 --> 00:09:00,160
that are usually in the wrong range and median range.

146
00:09:00,160 --> 00:09:05,160
So then there is real time and control decision making

147
00:09:05,580 --> 00:09:10,580
which is basically usually done currently in using

148
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:17,040
like expert decision and expert activities.

149
00:09:20,920 --> 00:09:25,920
There is an ongoing effort in the technology

150
00:09:26,840 --> 00:09:29,400
to improve the quality of control,

151
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:32,840
ongoing research to use real time data

152
00:09:32,880 --> 00:09:36,120
and improve the real time performance

153
00:09:36,120 --> 00:09:38,120
by using real time control.

154
00:09:38,120 --> 00:09:43,120
This includes revisions of the routes

155
00:09:43,440 --> 00:09:48,440
that could be the case in reaction to incidents

156
00:09:48,960 --> 00:09:51,400
and disruption situation.

157
00:09:51,400 --> 00:09:54,600
It could also relate to holding this

158
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:56,720
and dispatching decisions, thank you.

159
00:09:57,720 --> 00:10:02,720
That could actually be used to improve

160
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:06,720
the effective capacity of the system

161
00:10:06,720 --> 00:10:10,120
by trying to regulate,

162
00:10:10,120 --> 00:10:15,120
have a better frequency basically in the service.

163
00:10:18,720 --> 00:10:23,720
So similar to the typical planning problem,

164
00:10:24,720 --> 00:10:29,720
there are major elements in planning for public transportation

165
00:10:30,840 --> 00:10:33,520
and particularly in short term

166
00:10:33,520 --> 00:10:36,000
and short range decision making.

167
00:10:37,880 --> 00:10:41,800
It includes data collection similar to what you guys

168
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:45,120
have done in your homework assignment number two,

169
00:10:46,760 --> 00:10:48,200
collection of data on demand,

170
00:10:48,200 --> 00:10:50,800
collection of data on supply and interaction of supply

171
00:10:50,800 --> 00:10:53,640
and demand is an essential part to be able

172
00:10:53,640 --> 00:10:57,720
to make decisions and make the basically reliable

173
00:10:57,720 --> 00:11:00,040
and actionable intelligence

174
00:11:00,040 --> 00:11:02,760
for the decision making process.

175
00:11:02,760 --> 00:11:06,240
Then there should be like analysis

176
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:09,120
on identification of potential problems

177
00:11:09,120 --> 00:11:10,960
and identification of opportunities

178
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:12,680
to improve the situation.

179
00:11:12,680 --> 00:11:17,680
And then there is the creative part of design of options,

180
00:11:18,680 --> 00:11:21,960
solution and strategies to address the situation.

181
00:11:21,960 --> 00:11:26,960
And then typical to any economic analysis,

182
00:11:28,160 --> 00:11:32,320
there would be an element of cost and benefit estimation

183
00:11:32,320 --> 00:11:37,320
to be able to justify the project.

184
00:11:40,160 --> 00:11:43,760
So in terms of operational planning process

185
00:11:43,760 --> 00:11:48,760
or in another term, short range planning process,

186
00:11:48,840 --> 00:11:52,400
there are multiple stages involved in the work.

187
00:11:52,400 --> 00:11:57,400
And usually what happens is that in practice,

188
00:11:58,400 --> 00:12:03,400
it is proven that the planning procedure can be decomposed

189
00:12:04,520 --> 00:12:08,040
into sequential decision making processes.

190
00:12:08,040 --> 00:12:12,600
And that's, although it is possible

191
00:12:13,080 --> 00:12:16,000
to formulate all of these decisions

192
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,560
into one gigantic optimization problem

193
00:12:18,560 --> 00:12:22,200
and solve it for all the decision variables

194
00:12:22,200 --> 00:12:25,400
that you need to basically address.

195
00:12:25,400 --> 00:12:28,880
However, the practice has shown that there's a nice way

196
00:12:28,880 --> 00:12:33,000
of decomposing the process into some problems

197
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,840
and solve them sequentially in order to make sure

198
00:12:35,840 --> 00:12:40,840
that you're satisfying all the constraints

199
00:12:41,800 --> 00:12:46,800
to the accurate level and you're finding a realistic,

200
00:12:49,080 --> 00:12:53,140
optimal solution for your planning practice.

201
00:12:54,720 --> 00:12:58,840
Considerations regarding policy constraints

202
00:12:58,840 --> 00:13:03,840
and some of the economic concerns are usually difficult

203
00:13:06,120 --> 00:13:10,640
to formulate into optimization problems.

204
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:13,120
But there are guidelines, published guidelines

205
00:13:13,120 --> 00:13:18,040
by governmental agencies that you can basically follow

206
00:13:18,040 --> 00:13:21,480
and make sure that at least the top two level

207
00:13:21,480 --> 00:13:26,160
of the decision making processes and tasks here

208
00:13:26,160 --> 00:13:30,600
are basically done to the satisfactory level.

209
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:33,760
So let's go ahead and start and review these tasks.

210
00:13:33,760 --> 00:13:37,080
There are lectures in this course that are dealing with

211
00:13:37,080 --> 00:13:42,080
each one of these tasks and components in planning.

212
00:13:42,640 --> 00:13:46,720
But in this lecture, I just briefly go over

213
00:13:46,720 --> 00:13:51,520
these different components as an introductory part

214
00:13:51,520 --> 00:13:54,920
of the course.

215
00:13:55,920 --> 00:14:00,920
So we will have, for every optimization problem

216
00:14:02,480 --> 00:14:06,080
or in this case, planning process,

217
00:14:06,080 --> 00:14:10,720
you have a set of inputs, constraints, basically,

218
00:14:10,720 --> 00:14:12,360
that you need to satisfy.

219
00:14:12,360 --> 00:14:14,800
And then there is an objective function

220
00:14:14,800 --> 00:14:16,160
that you need to optimize.

221
00:14:16,160 --> 00:14:21,160
So the first component of the planning practice here

222
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:27,960
is bus route design.

223
00:14:27,960 --> 00:14:31,880
This includes design of layout of the routes

224
00:14:31,880 --> 00:14:35,680
and layout of the stops.

225
00:14:35,680 --> 00:14:40,000
And basically the input into this task includes

226
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:43,600
constraints that are related to right of way,

227
00:14:43,600 --> 00:14:47,000
roadway constraints, like if there's a river

228
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:48,880
in your network, there's this connectivity,

229
00:14:48,880 --> 00:14:50,480
there are bridges, tunnels.

230
00:14:50,480 --> 00:14:54,160
So these are the kind of constraints at network level

231
00:14:54,160 --> 00:14:56,240
that you would like to satisfy.

232
00:14:56,240 --> 00:14:59,000
And there's also demand constraints in the network

233
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,440
that you need to consider for this task.

234
00:15:02,440 --> 00:15:06,120
Like you have a sense of what's the demand

235
00:15:06,120 --> 00:15:09,520
from certain suburb to the downtown area.

236
00:15:09,520 --> 00:15:13,400
And you basically would like to have a route system

237
00:15:13,400 --> 00:15:18,120
that can address that demand and satisfy that demand.

238
00:15:18,120 --> 00:15:22,840
Then the solution to the design component

239
00:15:22,840 --> 00:15:26,000
and that task out of routes and layout of stops

240
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:30,640
that are basically resulting from the first component.

241
00:15:30,720 --> 00:15:33,520
And then it will be the set of routes

242
00:15:33,520 --> 00:15:37,680
and the stops will be input into the second task,

243
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:40,560
the task which is setting type timetables.

244
00:15:40,560 --> 00:15:45,160
This task includes finding frequencies for the routes

245
00:15:45,160 --> 00:15:48,760
and from that identifying and defining

246
00:15:48,760 --> 00:15:52,280
the basically generating the timetables

247
00:15:52,280 --> 00:15:54,440
based on frequencies.

248
00:15:54,440 --> 00:15:59,240
There are usually considerations related to policy headways,

249
00:15:59,240 --> 00:16:02,880
policy frequencies and capacity frequencies

250
00:16:02,880 --> 00:16:07,200
that need to be addressed in this task.

251
00:16:07,200 --> 00:16:12,200
But again, this task is also somehow dealt with

252
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:15,480
using the existing guidelines

253
00:16:15,480 --> 00:16:18,440
and just satisfying these guidelines.

254
00:16:18,440 --> 00:16:22,080
The input and other constraints that are considered

255
00:16:22,080 --> 00:16:24,960
for this task would be level of service guidelines

256
00:16:24,960 --> 00:16:29,040
and again, the demand constraints in the network.

257
00:16:30,360 --> 00:16:32,600
Then from the set of timetables,

258
00:16:32,600 --> 00:16:37,600
what will be resulted here is the departure times

259
00:16:37,600 --> 00:16:40,520
that are scheduled for the service.

260
00:16:40,520 --> 00:16:45,520
So the first two components of row one and row two

261
00:16:45,880 --> 00:16:49,600
basically are dealing with satisfying the demand

262
00:16:49,600 --> 00:16:51,280
and satisfying the guidelines.

263
00:16:51,280 --> 00:16:55,480
But once you go beyond the first two components,

264
00:16:55,480 --> 00:16:58,400
it becomes to the level that you know kind of

265
00:16:58,400 --> 00:17:01,040
what's the service you want to deliver

266
00:17:01,040 --> 00:17:03,160
and then you're dealing with tactical decision

267
00:17:03,160 --> 00:17:07,160
how you can optimize the cost of delivering this service

268
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:10,080
and improve efficiency.

269
00:17:10,080 --> 00:17:14,520
So the third row here basically deals with

270
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:18,840
the departure times that are given for all the routes

271
00:17:18,880 --> 00:17:22,480
in the network and for all times of day.

272
00:17:22,480 --> 00:17:25,240
And then what you need to do is how you want to

273
00:17:25,240 --> 00:17:30,240
allocate your resources in terms of allocate the vehicles

274
00:17:31,040 --> 00:17:35,760
to the system to make sure that that service

275
00:17:35,760 --> 00:17:39,520
is going to be delivered on time.

276
00:17:40,600 --> 00:17:45,280
Then so that would be the task of vehicle scheduling,

277
00:17:45,280 --> 00:17:47,080
input into that task or constraints

278
00:17:47,080 --> 00:17:52,080
that are running times on different segments of the route

279
00:17:53,320 --> 00:17:56,040
and also the departure times that are given

280
00:17:56,040 --> 00:17:59,560
from upper level decision making process

281
00:17:59,560 --> 00:18:02,760
and it will eventually result into a schedule

282
00:18:02,760 --> 00:18:06,960
of given buses in terms of what the specific trips

283
00:18:06,960 --> 00:18:09,840
they need to serve and what's the sequence of these trips.

284
00:18:11,040 --> 00:18:14,200
Then the output from that decision

285
00:18:14,240 --> 00:18:17,880
with the vehicle schedules into

286
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,520
the task of scheduling for drivers.

287
00:18:22,520 --> 00:18:26,960
This task also include input from operator

288
00:18:26,960 --> 00:18:30,640
and basically work union constraints

289
00:18:32,040 --> 00:18:36,120
like maximum shift hours and for example,

290
00:18:36,120 --> 00:18:39,720
like break times and lunch times

291
00:18:39,720 --> 00:18:42,120
and maximum separation between two shifts

292
00:18:42,120 --> 00:18:45,360
and the rules and regulations that are related to that

293
00:18:45,360 --> 00:18:47,320
and this task is again,

294
00:18:47,320 --> 00:18:50,800
is a task of allocating resources

295
00:18:50,800 --> 00:18:55,240
to minimize the cost of scheduling drivers

296
00:18:55,240 --> 00:18:58,000
to deliver the service

297
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,680
and the result would be the schedules for the crew.

298
00:19:03,720 --> 00:19:07,680
So then these decisions can be looked at

299
00:19:07,680 --> 00:19:09,840
in another way too,

300
00:19:09,840 --> 00:19:13,160
like we can further classify these decisions

301
00:19:13,160 --> 00:19:15,400
into service planning decisions

302
00:19:15,400 --> 00:19:17,160
and operational planning decisions.

303
00:19:18,040 --> 00:19:20,880
Service planning decisions would be the decisions

304
00:19:20,880 --> 00:19:23,040
that define service as it is perceived

305
00:19:23,040 --> 00:19:26,720
and understood by public.

306
00:19:26,720 --> 00:19:29,200
This is basically the part of service

307
00:19:29,200 --> 00:19:31,080
that is visible to the public

308
00:19:32,400 --> 00:19:35,680
and then there are decisions that are related to operations

309
00:19:35,680 --> 00:19:38,560
which basically defines how operations occur

310
00:19:38,560 --> 00:19:40,320
to produce the service.

311
00:19:40,320 --> 00:19:43,960
This is somehow a black box to the users,

312
00:19:43,960 --> 00:19:45,360
I mean to the passengers

313
00:19:46,800 --> 00:19:48,800
and basically, yeah, yeah,

314
00:19:48,800 --> 00:19:53,240
basically the passengers should not really care

315
00:19:53,240 --> 00:19:55,120
what's the vehicle schedule

316
00:19:55,120 --> 00:19:56,960
and then what's the crew schedule

317
00:19:56,960 --> 00:19:58,960
that is assigned to the trip

318
00:19:58,960 --> 00:20:02,280
as long as trip is being delivered as it is scheduled.

319
00:20:03,080 --> 00:20:08,080
So in terms of other characteristics of these decisions,

320
00:20:08,280 --> 00:20:12,880
we basically, for network design,

321
00:20:12,880 --> 00:20:16,200
frequency setting, timetable development,

322
00:20:16,200 --> 00:20:18,920
vehicle scheduling and crew scheduling,

323
00:20:18,920 --> 00:20:23,920
there is the decisions that are involved in planning

324
00:20:23,920 --> 00:20:27,920
basically starting from top to bottom,

325
00:20:27,920 --> 00:20:29,600
frequency of decisions,

326
00:20:29,640 --> 00:20:32,160
basically are increased

327
00:20:32,160 --> 00:20:37,160
and the scope of decision making into the future

328
00:20:37,360 --> 00:20:41,840
is basically become limited and smaller.

329
00:20:44,360 --> 00:20:49,360
And also, the decision making in the network design,

330
00:20:51,400 --> 00:20:53,400
frequency setting and timetable

331
00:20:53,400 --> 00:20:58,320
is basically made based on the network design

332
00:20:59,160 --> 00:21:02,640
and basically made based on guidelines and standards

333
00:21:02,640 --> 00:21:05,920
and decision making on the board level,

334
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:08,840
vehicle scheduling and crew scheduling is made

335
00:21:08,840 --> 00:21:11,640
usually based on optimization of cost

336
00:21:11,640 --> 00:21:16,640
and then given the nature of the decision making,

337
00:21:17,400 --> 00:21:19,440
the tools that are used

338
00:21:19,440 --> 00:21:21,160
and the techniques that are used

339
00:21:21,160 --> 00:21:25,120
for solving these planning decision making processes

340
00:21:25,120 --> 00:21:30,120
are basically ranging from judgment and manual analysis

341
00:21:33,160 --> 00:21:36,120
that is more dominated into computer tools

342
00:21:36,120 --> 00:21:38,680
that are basically designed

343
00:21:38,680 --> 00:21:43,240
to solve optimization problems for minimizing the cost.

344
00:21:45,760 --> 00:21:50,760
So similar to the classical evaluation structure,

345
00:21:51,760 --> 00:21:56,080
when you're dealing with planning questions

346
00:21:56,080 --> 00:22:00,480
and planning projects or planning alternatives,

347
00:22:01,400 --> 00:22:04,400
there's this structure that starts with goals,

348
00:22:04,400 --> 00:22:07,480
objective measures and standards or guidelines.

349
00:22:07,480 --> 00:22:11,360
So let's just make a quick practice

350
00:22:12,560 --> 00:22:17,560
and define a goal and then define objectives for that goal

351
00:22:17,720 --> 00:22:20,000
and define measures based on that

352
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:24,280
and see how a standard can be defined

353
00:22:29,920 --> 00:22:33,920
to make that planning decision acceptable.

354
00:22:37,160 --> 00:22:41,120
So let's define a goal.

355
00:22:41,120 --> 00:22:46,120
So does anyone have any suggestion?

356
00:22:51,640 --> 00:22:56,640
What is an important goal for public transportation system?

357
00:22:59,400 --> 00:23:00,640
Maximize ridership?

358
00:23:00,640 --> 00:23:02,920
Maximize ridership, yes.

359
00:23:02,920 --> 00:23:07,920
So let's say we want to maximize ridership.

360
00:23:08,920 --> 00:23:11,600
That's your research problem, right?

361
00:23:11,600 --> 00:23:12,440
Yeah.

362
00:23:12,440 --> 00:23:13,960
It's just the goal to that one.

363
00:23:13,960 --> 00:23:14,800
Right.

364
00:23:16,320 --> 00:23:18,920
So then in terms of objectives,

365
00:23:18,920 --> 00:23:22,520
what would be an objective that we can define for this?

366
00:23:25,840 --> 00:23:30,840
I would say probably the peak hour demand

367
00:23:31,840 --> 00:23:33,880
could be one objective

368
00:23:33,880 --> 00:23:36,720
or peak hour ridership in certain corridor.

369
00:23:36,720 --> 00:23:41,720
Let's say peak hour ridership

370
00:23:46,280 --> 00:23:47,800
per weekday, per month.

371
00:23:57,000 --> 00:24:00,160
What peak hour ridership would be the objective here

372
00:24:00,160 --> 00:24:01,920
and then the measure that you would define

373
00:24:01,920 --> 00:24:06,200
would be peak hour ridership per weekday, per month.

374
00:24:06,200 --> 00:24:11,200
And then the standard that you want to define

375
00:24:16,360 --> 00:24:19,520
would be something that would also,

376
00:24:19,520 --> 00:24:23,600
something that not only captures the demand side,

377
00:24:23,600 --> 00:24:26,120
but also the supply of the system too.

378
00:24:26,120 --> 00:24:31,120
So you would come up with probably like, I would like...

379
00:24:31,120 --> 00:24:36,120
Vehicle riders for mile, for hour.

380
00:24:36,120 --> 00:24:36,960
Yeah, exactly.

381
00:24:36,960 --> 00:24:40,240
Or let's say like 60% of capacity.

382
00:24:45,760 --> 00:24:47,720
Your standard in this case would be,

383
00:24:49,280 --> 00:24:53,480
your peak hour ridership should be greater than 60%

384
00:24:54,640 --> 00:24:56,880
of capacity.

385
00:24:56,880 --> 00:24:59,600
So this was just an example that we tried to work out

386
00:24:59,600 --> 00:25:03,240
one practice for evaluation structure.

387
00:25:03,240 --> 00:25:04,640
But let me give you another example

388
00:25:04,640 --> 00:25:09,640
that is actually used in the practice.

389
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:15,800
So I assume that we are considering the goal of

390
00:25:17,440 --> 00:25:20,120
reliability in the transit system.

391
00:25:20,120 --> 00:25:24,240
The goal of reliability would be considered

392
00:25:25,120 --> 00:25:26,880
one of the main goals because

393
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:32,000
if we have a reliable public transport system,

394
00:25:34,960 --> 00:25:39,960
you can secure and you can guarantee a body of demand

395
00:25:42,120 --> 00:25:46,960
in your network that can rely on your transit system

396
00:25:46,960 --> 00:25:51,960
and basically adapt to the lifestyle of using public transport.

397
00:25:57,760 --> 00:26:02,760
And that's beneficial to the system from different perspectives.

398
00:26:07,080 --> 00:26:09,680
So the objective that we can rely,

399
00:26:09,680 --> 00:26:12,480
that we can define for reliability in this case

400
00:26:12,480 --> 00:26:17,160
would be possibly, can you come up with some objective

401
00:26:17,160 --> 00:26:22,160
that we can define with the goal of improving reliability?

402
00:26:23,640 --> 00:26:25,000
On time performance, yes.

403
00:26:26,880 --> 00:26:31,880
So on time performance would be the objective

404
00:26:34,960 --> 00:26:38,880
and then we need to define a measure for this

405
00:26:38,880 --> 00:26:42,760
to be able to measure that objective

406
00:26:42,760 --> 00:26:46,040
for each one of the scenarios

407
00:26:46,040 --> 00:26:50,360
and for each one of the alternative plans.

408
00:26:50,360 --> 00:26:53,480
So what would be a measure for on time performance?

409
00:26:53,480 --> 00:26:58,480
So let's say percentage of on time arrivals.

410
00:27:13,600 --> 00:27:17,160
So we would say percentage of on time arrivals

411
00:27:17,160 --> 00:27:19,520
and we would define on time arrivals

412
00:27:19,520 --> 00:27:24,520
to be between zero and five minutes of a schedule.

413
00:27:27,680 --> 00:27:32,600
And then the standard that many of the agencies use

414
00:27:32,600 --> 00:27:37,600
to measure this would be 90 or 95% basically.

415
00:27:37,600 --> 00:27:42,600
So this way the transit agency can go ahead

416
00:27:52,840 --> 00:27:57,840
and evaluate the performance of the operators of the system

417
00:27:58,480 --> 00:28:02,520
who basically are running some part of the service

418
00:28:02,520 --> 00:28:04,560
and come back to them and say,

419
00:28:04,560 --> 00:28:08,560
okay, your performance in terms of reliability

420
00:28:08,560 --> 00:28:10,840
has been acceptable or not, please.

421
00:28:10,840 --> 00:28:14,080
So should the standard also include something

422
00:28:14,080 --> 00:28:18,200
like what is the gain over the previous standard?

423
00:28:18,200 --> 00:28:22,040
So for example, if the goal was the library

424
00:28:22,040 --> 00:28:24,880
for the previous phase of planning as well

425
00:28:24,880 --> 00:28:27,920
and the library was somewhere around 80%

426
00:28:27,920 --> 00:28:31,480
and now the goal is to achieve a 10% gain

427
00:28:31,600 --> 00:28:33,680
compared to the previous one.

428
00:28:33,680 --> 00:28:35,000
Right.

429
00:28:35,000 --> 00:28:35,960
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

430
00:28:35,960 --> 00:28:40,960
So another measure would be percentage improvement

431
00:28:41,400 --> 00:28:43,280
as compared to baseline.

432
00:28:43,280 --> 00:28:46,840
And then the transit agency can define a standard

433
00:28:46,840 --> 00:28:50,160
of at least 10% improvement to the baseline

434
00:28:50,160 --> 00:28:53,480
should be required for reliability.

435
00:28:53,480 --> 00:28:54,820
Thank you.

436
00:28:54,820 --> 00:28:59,820
So then one other interesting thing

437
00:29:01,680 --> 00:29:05,840
is the point that actually comes to mind

438
00:29:05,840 --> 00:29:08,240
looking at the existing guidelines

439
00:29:08,240 --> 00:29:13,240
is that on time arrivals are usually defined for zero to five.

440
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:17,640
That basically means that you consider a bus

441
00:29:17,640 --> 00:29:20,920
that is late by two minutes to be on time,

442
00:29:20,920 --> 00:29:23,520
but the bus that is early by one minute

443
00:29:23,520 --> 00:29:25,280
is not considered to be on time.

444
00:29:25,280 --> 00:29:28,520
Does anyone have any comments about this?

445
00:29:28,520 --> 00:29:31,040
Why do we consider,

446
00:29:31,040 --> 00:29:34,220
why we do not consider early arrivals on time?

447
00:29:35,400 --> 00:29:39,000
Miss connections for people who expected

448
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:42,080
to be there at a certain time, but they left.

449
00:29:42,080 --> 00:29:43,720
Yeah, yeah.

450
00:29:43,720 --> 00:29:48,720
So it can be proved that based on typical assumptions

451
00:29:50,280 --> 00:29:55,280
about passenger arrivals, early arrivals of buses

452
00:29:55,840 --> 00:30:00,840
and service is usually more negative as late arrivals.

453
00:30:01,960 --> 00:30:03,560
Let's make an example here.

454
00:30:03,560 --> 00:30:08,560
For example, let's assume under uniform arrivals

455
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:14,080
of passengers, let's assume if this is my scheduled

456
00:30:14,080 --> 00:30:17,440
arrivals over the axis of time,

457
00:30:17,440 --> 00:30:21,280
and this is my number of passengers waiting

458
00:30:21,280 --> 00:30:26,280
in the bus stop under uniform arrivals,

459
00:30:34,280 --> 00:30:37,080
accumulation of passengers that are waiting at the stop.

460
00:30:42,920 --> 00:30:46,480
And then what happens is that if one of these buses

461
00:30:46,480 --> 00:30:51,480
are early, let's say it's basically arrives at this time.

462
00:30:53,120 --> 00:30:55,440
What happens is that the amount of time

463
00:30:55,440 --> 00:30:59,480
that we're saving due to this arrival

464
00:30:59,480 --> 00:31:03,440
is basically less than the amount of time

465
00:31:03,440 --> 00:31:08,440
that will be added to our waiting time for the next trip.

466
00:31:09,760 --> 00:31:11,120
And that's because a number of people

467
00:31:11,120 --> 00:31:15,120
who are experiencing this is a little higher,

468
00:31:15,160 --> 00:31:17,800
number of people that are experiencing

469
00:31:17,800 --> 00:31:21,720
the additional waiting time is higher

470
00:31:21,720 --> 00:31:24,480
than number of people who are experiencing

471
00:31:24,480 --> 00:31:29,480
the early arrival and a cut to the waiting time.

472
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:35,000
So if you compare these two areas,

473
00:31:35,880 --> 00:31:40,880
that basically tells you you're basically having a loss

474
00:31:41,680 --> 00:31:43,880
or having an increase in waiting time.

475
00:31:43,880 --> 00:31:46,400
If your service is early.

476
00:31:46,400 --> 00:31:48,280
And then the situation becomes worse

477
00:31:49,200 --> 00:31:50,520
in the case that you suggested

478
00:31:50,520 --> 00:31:54,480
when people are coordinating with the system.

479
00:31:54,480 --> 00:31:57,320
For example, it is assumed that,

480
00:31:57,320 --> 00:32:01,760
and it can actually be proven by looking at the data too,

481
00:32:01,760 --> 00:32:06,440
that people make the transition to coordinated behavior

482
00:32:06,440 --> 00:32:08,840
and coordination with the schedule

483
00:32:08,840 --> 00:32:13,120
as the frequency of service increases.

484
00:32:13,120 --> 00:32:17,720
For example, for a service that is scheduled

485
00:32:17,720 --> 00:32:22,400
to be delivered every half hour or every one hour,

486
00:32:22,400 --> 00:32:25,600
it is expected that passengers are coordinating

487
00:32:25,600 --> 00:32:29,360
with the system and with the coordinating,

488
00:32:29,360 --> 00:32:33,560
there are arrivals with the departure of the buses.

489
00:32:34,520 --> 00:32:39,520
But when it comes to more frequent service

490
00:32:39,520 --> 00:32:44,520
then we will have more uniform arrivals.

491
00:32:45,520 --> 00:32:47,160
So in that case, what we would observe

492
00:32:47,160 --> 00:32:49,160
is that passengers arrivals would resemble

493
00:32:49,160 --> 00:32:50,360
something like this.

494
00:32:55,440 --> 00:33:00,120
Well, it has to go up to the original case.

495
00:33:01,400 --> 00:33:06,400
And then in that case, if your service is early

496
00:33:06,800 --> 00:33:08,720
by five or 10 minutes,

497
00:33:08,720 --> 00:33:13,160
you would have the main bulk of your passengers

498
00:33:13,160 --> 00:33:17,520
who need to wait another headway basically.

499
00:33:17,520 --> 00:33:21,960
So this should be actually added to your computation too.

500
00:33:24,400 --> 00:33:26,000
Is there any questions about this?

501
00:33:29,560 --> 00:33:30,400
Okay.

502
00:33:31,000 --> 00:33:36,000
So in terms of the advantage of guidelines

503
00:33:39,800 --> 00:33:44,560
in transit planning, one of the main purposes

504
00:33:44,560 --> 00:33:48,200
of these guidelines is to communicate to public

505
00:33:48,200 --> 00:33:51,400
the rationale and the logic behind your decision-making

506
00:33:51,400 --> 00:33:54,960
and behind your allocation of resources to the system.

507
00:33:54,960 --> 00:33:58,480
It also gives you a consistent and fair basis

508
00:33:58,480 --> 00:34:03,480
for decision-making and it somehow gives you a tool

509
00:34:05,080 --> 00:34:08,080
to justify your decisions and also a tool

510
00:34:08,080 --> 00:34:10,720
to make sure that your decisions are fair

511
00:34:10,720 --> 00:34:13,840
and justified in nature.

512
00:34:13,840 --> 00:34:18,360
It also gives you the opportunity to balance

513
00:34:18,360 --> 00:34:22,080
the improvement and the investments to the network

514
00:34:22,080 --> 00:34:26,120
and gain a uniform level of service improvement

515
00:34:26,120 --> 00:34:27,640
for efficient service.

516
00:34:29,440 --> 00:34:33,800
So the guidelines that are existing in the literature

517
00:34:33,800 --> 00:34:37,400
and in the practice basically, in the state of practice

518
00:34:37,400 --> 00:34:42,400
are dealing with the design of service

519
00:34:42,720 --> 00:34:44,600
and with the delivery of service.

520
00:34:44,600 --> 00:34:49,600
So the design of service basically dealing with variables

521
00:34:51,440 --> 00:34:55,880
that are related to spacing and density of stops,

522
00:34:56,520 --> 00:35:00,840
spacing and density of trips and frequency of the service

523
00:35:00,840 --> 00:35:03,720
as it is designed and as it is in the schedule.

524
00:35:03,720 --> 00:35:05,480
So there are guidelines related to that

525
00:35:05,480 --> 00:35:09,120
and there's also guidelines related to how you deliver

526
00:35:09,120 --> 00:35:12,800
or how the transit agency delivers the design service

527
00:35:12,800 --> 00:35:16,280
and how the performance actually took place.

528
00:35:18,200 --> 00:35:22,080
So the main factors of service design

529
00:35:22,120 --> 00:35:26,480
and service quality from the passenger's perspective

530
00:35:27,520 --> 00:35:31,120
are basically reported by transit capacity

531
00:35:31,120 --> 00:35:32,920
and quality of service manual

532
00:35:36,360 --> 00:35:38,360
of the Transportation Research Board

533
00:35:38,360 --> 00:35:43,360
and it basically includes frequency of the service

534
00:35:45,080 --> 00:35:50,080
as one of the main aspects or factors

535
00:35:50,080 --> 00:35:52,400
that are defining the quality of service,

536
00:35:52,400 --> 00:35:55,040
waiting time, reliability and access.

537
00:35:55,040 --> 00:35:57,880
This is access over time and also access over space.

538
00:35:59,120 --> 00:36:02,520
So why do we again have both frequency

539
00:36:02,520 --> 00:36:04,880
and waiting time in here?

540
00:36:04,880 --> 00:36:09,880
So if frequency and waiting time are kind of interrelated,

541
00:36:11,320 --> 00:36:16,320
how come there are two separate items here on the list here?

542
00:36:17,320 --> 00:36:20,160
Does anyone have any suggestions about this?

543
00:36:30,280 --> 00:36:35,280
So let me go ahead and explain how I understand this.

544
00:36:37,720 --> 00:36:40,160
Frequency of the service is important

545
00:36:40,160 --> 00:36:45,160
in terms of not only in terms of the waiting time

546
00:36:45,560 --> 00:36:47,520
that could be associated with that,

547
00:36:47,520 --> 00:36:51,440
but also when it comes to lower frequency system

548
00:36:51,440 --> 00:36:53,160
and longer headways,

549
00:36:55,480 --> 00:36:58,320
the waiting time is not a function of frequency anymore.

550
00:36:58,320 --> 00:37:03,120
It basically, as we discussed,

551
00:37:03,120 --> 00:37:08,120
the waiting time could actually be independent of frequency.

552
00:37:08,320 --> 00:37:10,320
However, in those cases,

553
00:37:10,320 --> 00:37:12,240
there is a burden of coordination

554
00:37:12,240 --> 00:37:14,760
that is imposed to the passenger.

555
00:37:14,800 --> 00:37:17,920
So if you have to coordinate your arrival,

556
00:37:17,920 --> 00:37:21,280
for example, for your in the morning from home to work,

557
00:37:21,280 --> 00:37:24,200
then there is some sort of inconvenience

558
00:37:24,200 --> 00:37:26,880
that is imposed to you to basically

559
00:37:28,360 --> 00:37:32,960
to make it to your coordinator the schedule.

560
00:37:32,960 --> 00:37:34,960
And then waiting time is also important

561
00:37:34,960 --> 00:37:39,960
because if the service is frequent,

562
00:37:40,960 --> 00:37:45,560
but at different ranges of frequency,

563
00:37:45,560 --> 00:37:47,680
you may experience different waiting time

564
00:37:47,680 --> 00:37:51,760
if the process of arrivals of passenger

565
00:37:51,760 --> 00:37:54,960
is assumed to be around the process.

566
00:37:54,960 --> 00:37:56,800
Then reliability, as we discussed,

567
00:37:56,800 --> 00:38:00,400
is one of the important features

568
00:38:00,400 --> 00:38:02,760
and one of the important aspects of the service.

569
00:38:04,200 --> 00:38:09,200
It usually is measured based on on-time arrivals of the service

570
00:38:09,440 --> 00:38:16,440
and then the next aspect that is important

571
00:38:16,440 --> 00:38:19,920
is access to origins and destinations.

572
00:38:20,920 --> 00:38:23,280
This access can be interpreted in two ways.

573
00:38:24,440 --> 00:38:26,600
We can look at access in terms of space

574
00:38:26,600 --> 00:38:27,800
and in terms of time.

575
00:38:27,800 --> 00:38:32,320
Access in terms of space is closeness to the bus stops

576
00:38:32,320 --> 00:38:36,520
and closeness to the service from origin

577
00:38:36,520 --> 00:38:37,920
and from the destination.

578
00:38:37,960 --> 00:38:40,840
Access in terms of time would be a span of service

579
00:38:40,840 --> 00:38:43,000
when you like to travel

580
00:38:43,000 --> 00:38:45,280
and basically what is the service time

581
00:38:45,280 --> 00:38:48,600
and available times of service.

582
00:38:51,280 --> 00:38:52,640
So yeah, as I mentioned,

583
00:38:52,640 --> 00:38:56,040
most agencies have guidelines covering a span of service.

584
00:38:56,040 --> 00:39:01,040
For example, MBTA has this guideline

585
00:39:01,320 --> 00:39:06,320
that for weekdays, you need to have service

586
00:39:06,320 --> 00:39:10,480
from all suburbs into the CBD area

587
00:39:11,440 --> 00:39:16,440
with that at least like at latest arrives at 7 a.m.

588
00:39:16,920 --> 00:39:21,000
So you need to basically be able to cover

589
00:39:21,000 --> 00:39:22,960
between 7 a.m. and 6 p.m.

590
00:39:24,040 --> 00:39:28,800
as your services span in MBTA.

591
00:39:28,800 --> 00:39:33,120
For TransLink, it's also somehow the same.

592
00:39:33,120 --> 00:39:35,800
You need to basically minimum service guidelines

593
00:39:35,800 --> 00:39:38,840
to ensure that 95% of trips listed

594
00:39:38,840 --> 00:39:43,600
can be completed in the time span given below.

595
00:39:43,600 --> 00:39:47,240
Like from any point to downtown Vancouver,

596
00:39:47,240 --> 00:39:50,480
you need to have service that can be finished

597
00:39:50,480 --> 00:39:54,440
by can actually arrive in downtown Vancouver by 7 a.m.

598
00:39:54,440 --> 00:39:56,160
for people who like to start,

599
00:39:56,160 --> 00:39:59,080
should start their work at 7 a.m.

600
00:39:59,080 --> 00:40:03,840
And then there's also trips in the evening

601
00:40:03,880 --> 00:40:08,360
and it basically says earliest departure time

602
00:40:08,360 --> 00:40:10,720
of last transit trip in the evening

603
00:40:10,720 --> 00:40:13,520
from downtown Vancouver to any town center

604
00:40:13,520 --> 00:40:16,440
needs to be midnight for people who basically

605
00:40:16,440 --> 00:40:21,040
would like to enjoy the nightlife in the downtown area

606
00:40:21,040 --> 00:40:23,440
and then use transit to go back home.

607
00:40:26,600 --> 00:40:28,680
So in terms of route design

608
00:40:28,680 --> 00:40:33,120
and in terms of layout of routes and stops,

609
00:40:34,120 --> 00:40:38,320
there are also guidelines that are addressing this decision.

610
00:40:38,320 --> 00:40:41,440
MBTA has a policy objective to provide transit service

611
00:40:41,440 --> 00:40:45,680
within walking distance, which is defined a quarter mile

612
00:40:45,680 --> 00:40:48,000
of all residents that are living in area

613
00:40:48,000 --> 00:40:51,320
with population densities greater than 5,000 people

614
00:40:51,320 --> 00:40:53,240
per square mile.

615
00:40:53,240 --> 00:40:58,120
That's basically an interesting guideline.

616
00:40:58,120 --> 00:41:00,760
It considers two different variables.

617
00:41:00,760 --> 00:41:02,560
One is the population density

618
00:41:02,560 --> 00:41:05,840
and the other one is the density of the stops

619
00:41:05,840 --> 00:41:10,840
or vicinity or walkability of transit

620
00:41:11,520 --> 00:41:16,240
in areas that are populated with densities

621
00:41:16,240 --> 00:41:18,240
greater than 5,000 per square mile.

622
00:41:20,400 --> 00:41:24,560
So in terms of comprehensiveness objective,

623
00:41:25,600 --> 00:41:30,080
TransLink basically has a very comprehensive guideline

624
00:41:30,080 --> 00:41:34,080
related to basically a set of different

625
00:41:35,160 --> 00:41:38,520
performance measures and goals.

626
00:41:38,520 --> 00:41:43,280
Let me read from these.

627
00:41:43,280 --> 00:41:46,480
At least 90% of our residents and employees

628
00:41:46,480 --> 00:41:49,240
in urbanized development areas should have a walk

629
00:41:49,240 --> 00:41:52,520
less than 450 meters to a bus stop.

630
00:41:52,520 --> 00:41:57,440
So this is related to access over space.

631
00:41:57,440 --> 00:41:58,840
98%

632
00:41:58,840 --> 00:42:01,360
when they say 90% of residents and employees

633
00:42:01,360 --> 00:42:05,000
basically they're looking at the person twice.

634
00:42:05,000 --> 00:42:07,240
The person is on one end of resident

635
00:42:07,240 --> 00:42:08,880
and on the other end of employee.

636
00:42:08,880 --> 00:42:10,240
Right, yes.

637
00:42:10,240 --> 00:42:11,600
So it's...

638
00:42:11,600 --> 00:42:16,200
Yeah, you want to consider both ends of the trip, right?

639
00:42:16,200 --> 00:42:19,120
And these percentages are a little tricky.

640
00:42:19,120 --> 00:42:21,320
Sometimes they're counting the number of people,

641
00:42:21,320 --> 00:42:22,240
number of residents.

642
00:42:22,240 --> 00:42:25,160
Sometimes they're counting OD pairs.

643
00:42:25,160 --> 00:42:27,200
Sometimes they're counting passengers.

644
00:42:27,240 --> 00:42:32,240
So it's a little tricky to interpret these correctly.

645
00:42:33,040 --> 00:42:36,040
So the second one basically says 98%

646
00:42:36,040 --> 00:42:38,000
of all peak period transit trips

647
00:42:38,000 --> 00:42:40,400
to and from downtown Vancouver should require

648
00:42:40,400 --> 00:42:43,600
no more than one transfer.

649
00:42:43,600 --> 00:42:47,760
That basically is 98% of people who are traveling

650
00:42:47,760 --> 00:42:51,440
from and to downtown to the suburbs.

651
00:42:51,440 --> 00:42:55,320
And so it may be a little difficult,

652
00:42:55,320 --> 00:42:59,440
especially in the absence of AFC data,

653
00:42:59,440 --> 00:43:02,600
automated fare collection system data,

654
00:43:02,600 --> 00:43:06,720
it can be a little tricky to measure these objectives.

655
00:43:07,600 --> 00:43:11,880
Usually what's usually done basically is people go out

656
00:43:11,880 --> 00:43:16,880
and basically, and ask from transit riders

657
00:43:17,000 --> 00:43:18,760
about their origins and destinations.

658
00:43:18,760 --> 00:43:21,520
It's called public transit origin destination survey

659
00:43:21,520 --> 00:43:23,840
that is usually done on board.

660
00:43:23,840 --> 00:43:24,840
Do you have a question?

661
00:43:25,320 --> 00:43:26,160
Okay.

662
00:43:28,320 --> 00:43:31,600
So the second measure here relates

663
00:43:31,600 --> 00:43:33,520
to connectivity in the network.

664
00:43:34,680 --> 00:43:39,080
The third one is 95% of all peak period transit trips

665
00:43:39,080 --> 00:43:41,720
to the nearest town center should require

666
00:43:41,720 --> 00:43:42,920
no more than one transfer.

667
00:43:42,920 --> 00:43:46,240
Again, this one is also related to connectivity

668
00:43:46,240 --> 00:43:47,720
of the network.

669
00:43:47,720 --> 00:43:51,640
And fourth one is all trips between one town center

670
00:43:51,640 --> 00:43:55,240
and adjacent town centers should require no transfers.

671
00:43:55,280 --> 00:43:59,520
Again, this one is a guideline on connectivity.

672
00:43:59,520 --> 00:44:04,520
And then again, the last one is also on connectivity.

673
00:44:04,840 --> 00:44:09,200
It basically requires that 95% of all peak period transit

674
00:44:09,200 --> 00:44:12,080
trips to major regional activity centers

675
00:44:12,080 --> 00:44:16,040
and gateways should require no more than two transfers.

676
00:44:19,240 --> 00:44:21,440
Okay, so any questions or comments about this?

677
00:44:21,440 --> 00:44:22,280
Yeah.

678
00:44:22,280 --> 00:44:24,760
In terms of one connectivity between one town center

679
00:44:24,760 --> 00:44:26,840
and adjacent town center,

680
00:44:26,840 --> 00:44:30,200
definition of the town varies from place to place.

681
00:44:30,200 --> 00:44:32,360
I know that specifically Vancouver has

682
00:44:33,360 --> 00:44:36,320
rather large suburbs, like suburbs of 100,000 people,

683
00:44:36,320 --> 00:44:37,280
200,000 people.

684
00:44:37,280 --> 00:44:41,920
So you actually have a few municipal entities

685
00:44:41,920 --> 00:44:43,520
for a large area.

686
00:44:43,520 --> 00:44:46,000
But you could have areas where that condition

687
00:44:46,000 --> 00:44:48,600
would be a little bit too strict if you have,

688
00:44:48,600 --> 00:44:49,720
let's say the Boston area,

689
00:44:49,720 --> 00:44:52,160
which has generally slightly smaller suburbs

690
00:44:52,760 --> 00:44:54,080
and more suburbs to connect.

691
00:44:54,080 --> 00:44:55,160
Right, yeah.

692
00:44:55,160 --> 00:44:58,320
So some of these may actually be a little tricky

693
00:44:58,320 --> 00:45:01,320
to measure and to quantify.

694
00:45:01,320 --> 00:45:02,600
Yeah, I know better to compare.

695
00:45:02,600 --> 00:45:04,120
Right.

696
00:45:04,120 --> 00:45:07,400
Yeah, when I see these numbers and I try to think

697
00:45:07,400 --> 00:45:12,400
about equity and the 10% that is not 450 from that.

698
00:45:13,560 --> 00:45:14,400
Yeah.

699
00:45:14,400 --> 00:45:15,240
Right.

700
00:45:15,240 --> 00:45:17,080
About 5% of it.

701
00:45:17,080 --> 00:45:19,120
So how do you navigate these numbers

702
00:45:19,120 --> 00:45:22,040
in the face of enough equity?

703
00:45:22,040 --> 00:45:24,920
Right, this is a really good point.

704
00:45:24,920 --> 00:45:27,200
Some of these percentages, I think,

705
00:45:27,200 --> 00:45:31,200
are related to random variables.

706
00:45:31,200 --> 00:45:33,440
And those are somehow acceptable

707
00:45:33,440 --> 00:45:36,000
because that's when you try to define

708
00:45:36,000 --> 00:45:37,600
a confidence interval.

709
00:45:37,600 --> 00:45:40,600
But when it comes to cases like this,

710
00:45:40,600 --> 00:45:43,400
it is not a random variable anymore.

711
00:45:43,400 --> 00:45:46,480
It's basically like a special variable

712
00:45:46,480 --> 00:45:51,480
that is constantly requires,

713
00:45:51,520 --> 00:45:53,280
like a certain amount of connections

714
00:45:53,280 --> 00:45:56,360
to the downtown area, right?

715
00:45:56,360 --> 00:46:00,480
So that may be one of the loopholes of this guideline.

716
00:46:00,480 --> 00:46:01,920
That's a really good point.

717
00:46:01,920 --> 00:46:06,920
And because one may argue that if 95% of my OD pairs

718
00:46:08,440 --> 00:46:12,120
are connected with the minimum connectivity requirement,

719
00:46:12,120 --> 00:46:14,200
what about the rest of 5%?

720
00:46:14,200 --> 00:46:18,120
Does it mean that all people who live in that 5% area

721
00:46:18,120 --> 00:46:23,120
will have to go through the unacceptable

722
00:46:23,280 --> 00:46:25,040
connectivity situation?

723
00:46:25,040 --> 00:46:26,480
So that's a valid point.

724
00:46:26,480 --> 00:46:28,080
And thank you for bringing that up.

725
00:46:28,080 --> 00:46:28,920
Right.

726
00:46:28,920 --> 00:46:31,320
Does they correctly achieve these goals

727
00:46:31,320 --> 00:46:35,400
as you kind of blocked the difficulty?

728
00:46:35,400 --> 00:46:36,240
Right.

729
00:46:37,280 --> 00:46:39,880
Yeah, well, these are basically,

730
00:46:39,880 --> 00:46:43,080
these are usually for projects and plans

731
00:46:43,080 --> 00:46:46,480
that are being proposed

732
00:46:47,240 --> 00:46:50,200
for like a small start projects

733
00:46:50,200 --> 00:46:53,360
or for improvement plans.

734
00:46:53,360 --> 00:46:57,120
So I think what they try to do is to make sure

735
00:46:57,120 --> 00:46:59,760
that the ones that are being accepted

736
00:46:59,760 --> 00:47:03,360
and being implemented would satisfy this.

737
00:47:03,360 --> 00:47:06,240
But I'm not really sure if the existing network

738
00:47:06,240 --> 00:47:08,520
satisfy all these conditions or not.

739
00:47:09,960 --> 00:47:12,720
Sorry, is transit the name of the agency that does?

740
00:47:12,720 --> 00:47:13,800
Yes, right.

741
00:47:13,800 --> 00:47:14,960
Yeah, right.

742
00:47:16,960 --> 00:47:18,920
Okay, so any other questions?

743
00:47:26,560 --> 00:47:30,440
So then TransLink guideline on root design

744
00:47:30,440 --> 00:47:32,560
and is very interesting too.

745
00:47:32,560 --> 00:47:35,960
They have like really interesting logic

746
00:47:35,960 --> 00:47:40,720
to validate the design and the improvements

747
00:47:40,720 --> 00:47:43,320
that are proposed for root design.

748
00:47:43,320 --> 00:47:45,640
So what it says is that deviations

749
00:47:45,640 --> 00:47:49,480
from the most direct route must have walking time savings

750
00:47:49,480 --> 00:47:52,160
for customers on the added route section

751
00:47:52,160 --> 00:47:55,640
greater than the increase in total travel time

752
00:47:55,640 --> 00:47:57,160
for true passengers.

753
00:47:57,160 --> 00:48:02,160
So does anyone have any interpretation,

754
00:48:03,880 --> 00:48:06,400
quick interpretation on this guideline?

755
00:48:09,000 --> 00:48:11,840
The only reason I do it is there's a major destination

756
00:48:11,840 --> 00:48:13,280
where people are on the right-hand block

757
00:48:13,280 --> 00:48:16,440
of the route, otherwise people are going straight.

758
00:48:16,440 --> 00:48:18,440
And what would be the aggregate time savings

759
00:48:18,440 --> 00:48:21,560
for people on the left-hand block?

760
00:48:21,560 --> 00:48:22,880
The second or last stop on the route,

761
00:48:22,880 --> 00:48:24,440
maybe you make that deviation

762
00:48:24,440 --> 00:48:25,840
in order for people on the left-hand block

763
00:48:25,840 --> 00:48:27,200
to be less than the traffic.

764
00:48:27,200 --> 00:48:28,800
Right, yeah.

765
00:48:28,800 --> 00:48:30,560
Right, you had a comment too?

766
00:48:32,360 --> 00:48:33,440
Right, yeah.

767
00:48:33,440 --> 00:48:38,440
So if you look at the problem or look at the situation

768
00:48:39,120 --> 00:48:40,680
from the perspective of system

769
00:48:40,680 --> 00:48:42,760
and from the perspective of total travel time

770
00:48:42,760 --> 00:48:46,920
in the system, what they basically are requiring

771
00:48:46,920 --> 00:48:51,240
is that, for example, let's assume that I have a suburb here

772
00:48:51,240 --> 00:48:53,280
and then this is my downtown area

773
00:48:53,280 --> 00:48:56,800
and there's a route here that has multiple stops

774
00:48:56,800 --> 00:48:59,600
and then there's another suburb here

775
00:48:59,600 --> 00:49:03,400
which in the original plan is being served by this route.

776
00:49:03,400 --> 00:49:08,400
However, it requires a walking link to different stops.

777
00:49:09,000 --> 00:49:11,520
What this guideline basically requires

778
00:49:11,520 --> 00:49:14,960
is that if the total travel time

779
00:49:14,960 --> 00:49:18,040
for all passengers who are traveling from here to here

780
00:49:18,040 --> 00:49:21,280
and for all that are traveling from here to here

781
00:49:21,280 --> 00:49:26,280
is going to be increased, then you cannot justify

782
00:49:27,240 --> 00:49:31,520
an additional detour here to serve this area.

783
00:49:32,400 --> 00:49:35,800
Meaning that if the total travel time saving

784
00:49:35,800 --> 00:49:40,800
that is done on the walking time of these people

785
00:49:40,960 --> 00:49:45,680
is not as much as total increase in travel time of people

786
00:49:45,680 --> 00:49:49,320
that are traveling from this suburb to this suburb,

787
00:49:49,320 --> 00:49:53,840
then you cannot justify the design or the reroute.

788
00:49:56,240 --> 00:49:57,080
Okay.

789
00:50:00,120 --> 00:50:05,120
So in terms of guidelines related to a schedule

790
00:50:05,120 --> 00:50:08,560
and guidelines related to frequency and timetable,

791
00:50:08,560 --> 00:50:13,560
we basically, in practice, there are two main components,

792
00:50:14,440 --> 00:50:19,440
component that is related to the policy of design

793
00:50:19,680 --> 00:50:22,320
and components that is related to the crowding situation.

794
00:50:22,320 --> 00:50:26,240
Policy of design basically dictate a minimum headway

795
00:50:26,240 --> 00:50:29,740
or basically a minimum frequency

796
00:50:29,740 --> 00:50:33,520
or a maximum headway for the service

797
00:50:33,520 --> 00:50:36,200
and that's basically the case

798
00:50:36,200 --> 00:50:39,480
when there's no crowding situation.

799
00:50:39,480 --> 00:50:44,480
However, in the main corridors and in peak directions,

800
00:50:44,680 --> 00:50:49,680
we usually would go beyond the service of policy headway

801
00:50:50,440 --> 00:50:54,800
because we need to accommodate the demand

802
00:50:54,800 --> 00:50:59,800
and that's basically how the maximum passenger

803
00:50:59,840 --> 00:51:04,600
crowding guides come into play.

804
00:51:04,600 --> 00:51:09,600
So in terms of the actual guideline on policy of headway

805
00:51:11,800 --> 00:51:16,800
for MBTA, we have maximum headway on all local bus routes

806
00:51:18,040 --> 00:51:21,400
should be 30 minutes in peak and 60 minutes at other times

807
00:51:21,400 --> 00:51:23,920
for express routes, there should be at least three trips

808
00:51:23,920 --> 00:51:25,060
in each peak period.

809
00:51:25,980 --> 00:51:30,980
So let's discuss a little bit more about this.

810
00:51:31,740 --> 00:51:35,380
Why do you think that we need to have a policy headway,

811
00:51:35,380 --> 00:51:37,180
maximum policy headway?

812
00:51:39,780 --> 00:51:43,140
If we are considering the demand

813
00:51:43,140 --> 00:51:46,220
and if we are trying to satisfy the demand

814
00:51:46,220 --> 00:51:51,220
by number of vehicles that we are assigning to the route,

815
00:51:52,180 --> 00:51:54,420
then why should we care about a minimum?

